WHY THIS EXISTS
-------------------------------------------------------------------

An iOS application cannot reach a phone except through a distribution
platform, and every such platform imposes terms on the people who
receive the software: a cap on the number of devices, a prohibition on
passing the binary on, and a requirement that it run only under the
platform's own signing and digital rights management.

Sections 4, 5, 6 and 10 of the License forbid conveying the Program
under exactly those conditions. Section 10 is the sharpest of them --
"you may not impose any further restrictions on the exercise of the
rights granted or affirmed under this License" -- and a distribution
platform's terms of service are further restrictions, imposed on the
recipient, as a condition of receiving the binary.

The practical effect is that GPL-3.0 software and the App Store are
mutually exclusive by default. This is not a theoretical concern:
Apple removed VLC from the App Store in January 2011 for precisely
this reason, after a complaint from one of its copyright holders.

This exception removes that one obstacle. It does nothing else, and
every other obligation of the License survives it untouched.


